# AI Code Review

Here are the main issues I found in the AI-generated Playwright script:

### 1. Unused Imports & Mixing Frameworks
The script imports `webdriver` from `selenium`, but this is a Playwright test. The import is never used. Mixing frameworks just creates confusion and adds unnecessary dependencies.

### 2. Hardcoded Sleeps
Using `time.sleep()` is a bad practice. It makes tests flaky and unnecessarily slow. Playwright has a built-in auto-wait feature, meaning it automatically waits for elements to be ready before interacting with them. We don't need manual sleeps.

### 3. Resource Leaks (No Context Manager)
The script starts the browser using `sync_playwright().start()`, but it never properly stops the Playwright process. If the test fails in the middle, the browser won't close. The correct way to handle this in Python is using a `with` statement so resources are cleaned up automatically.

### 4. Missing Assertions
The script locates `results` at the end but doesn't do anything with it. A test without an assertion isn't really a test. We need to verify that the search actually returned results.


### The Fixed Code

Here is the refactored version using Playwright best practices:

python:
from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright, expect

def test_search_functionality():
    # Use 'with' to ensure the browser closes even if the test fails
    with sync_playwright() as p:
        browser = p.chromium.launch()
        page = browser.new_page()
        
        page.goto("[https://example.com](https://example.com)")

        # Playwright waits automatically, so time.sleep() is removed
        page.locator("#search").fill("playwright testing")
        page.locator(".button").click()

        # Added an assertion to actually validate the test
        results = page.locator(".result-item")
        expect(results.first).to_be_visible()
